   "source": [
    "What dimensionality reduction using SVD does is project every data point onto a k-dimensional subspace with k singular vectors (corresponding to the k largest singular values) as bases.\n",
    "This subspace captures the major patterns/variances in the data that we care about.\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, non-anomalous data points (ones that generally correlate with the overall dataset) should not be affected a significant amount by the projection as \n",
    "most of its information will be stored in the singular values that haven't been truncated. Therefore the Euclidean distance between the original point and the projected point will be small. \n",
    "\n",
    "However, anomalous data points generally don't correlate with the overall dataset, and so rank truncation would remove a significant amount of information about the data point.\n",
    "Therefore, the Euclidean distance between the original point and the projected point will be great."
   ]
